  "finding": {
    "cap_was_one_guard": "Rows 5-8 previously raised inside the verifier. The only blocker was the Python and C guard on the fused rounded add/RMSNorm wrapper (hip_gfx1100/add+rmsnorm/gguf_f32_weight/rounded_bf16_out). Its device kernel launches one block per row with an identical 256-thread reduction tree, so it is row-count agnostic and the guard was narrower than every caller's declared 2-8 envelope.",
    "device_accept_commit_was_never_capped": "The earlier claim that the device accept/commit kernels also cap at four rows is wrong. The verifier binds positions from a device array (prepare_packed_decode_metadata_from_positions); the four-scalar position0..position3 ABI belongs to prepare_packed_decode_metadata, which has no in-tree runtime caller.",
    "cliff_at_five_rows": "Above four rows the IQ family leaves the rows 2-4 local32 verifier sibling and reverts to gguf_iq_dense_strict, which then owns 59.5-61.8% of verifier kernel time. Rows 5 costs 91.32 ms/step against 51.15 at rows 4, so 25% more rows costs 78% more time: 18.26 ms/row against 12.79. The strict kernel's cost is nearly row-count independent (52.8 / 54.0 / 57.9 ms/step at rows 5 / 6 / 8) because it is dominated by the per-step IQ weight stream, so rows 5 is the worst per-row point of the four.",
    "reachable_shape": "Budget 4 is the only newly reachable production shape: _GGUF_MTP_CANDIDATE_BUDGETS is (1,2,3,4), so rows 5 = one root row plus B4. Rows 6-8 need a budget policy that admits 5-7, which the declared envelope does not.",
    "default_path_unchanged": "max_candidate_budget defaults to 3 and candidate_budget to 1, so the measured B1-B3 protocol and every default route are unaffected. Budget 4 requires explicit opt-in."
  },
  "notes": [
    "This is attribution evidence. The per-step times are single-host single-GPU profiler runs at one prompt, not a paired throughput benchmark, and they carry no rate claim.",
    "The census bucket classifier leaves most kernel time in an 'other' bucket, so the per-family table understates the families it does not name; the top-kernels table is the reliable view and is what this artifact records."
  ]
